Yeah. The same signal can land differently in two people, and both can accurately report how it landed for them. Where you need more evidence is when you move from “That’s what it did to me” to “That’s what they meant” or “That’s what it did to everyone.”

Say someone jokes in a meeting, “Look who finally decided to speak.” One person hears an invitation to join in. Another hears a public dig. Someone else barely registers it. The person who felt singled out might know the speaker has made that joke at their expense three times already. Or the joke might touch an insecurity the speaker had no idea about. Both can be true at once.

There are three separate things to get sure about:

Question	What can help you answer it
How did it affect me?	Your own reaction. “I felt put on the spot.”
How did it affect other people?	Their accounts and what they did afterward. “Did that land as a joke to you?”
What did the speaker mean?	Their explanation, the context, and what they consistently do. “What did you mean by that?”

And being the only one who caught something can cut both ways. Maybe you supplied a meaning from your own history. Maybe you knew a piece of the history nobody else in the room knew. The question is what information did each person have that the others didn’t?

That’s where the circuitry analogy earns its keep. The same input can produce different outputs depending on what it’s connected to and what state the receiver is already in. With people, that includes their relationship to the speaker, their history, and what’s at stake in the room. Your reaction gives you real information about that encounter. Figuring out which part came from the words, which part came from the relationship, and which part came from what you brought into the room takes another step.